WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.374
Articulate 360 is best understood as an eLearning authoring ecosystem rather than

00:00:04.374 --> 00:00:06.511
a single magic button for training.

00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:10.429
It brings together tools for building courses, creating interactions, managing review,

00:00:10.429 --> 00:00:13.990
using media assets, and publishing learning content for online delivery.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:18.621
In a practical training team, that means one environment may support slide-based courses,

00:00:18.621 --> 00:00:21.470
responsive lessons, assessments, stakeholder review, and LMS-ready output.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.675
It helps to separate the parts of the ecosystem.

00:00:24.675 --> 00:00:28.237
Authoring tools are where the course is designed and built.

00:00:28.237 --> 00:00:30.730
Review tools support comments, approvals, and revisions.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.936
Media assets provide starting points for visuals, icons, templates,

00:00:33.936 --> 00:00:35.004
and character-style elements.

00:00:35.004 --> 00:00:37.853
Publishing formats control how the course is delivered,

00:00:37.853 --> 00:00:42.484
while the learning platform or LMS handles launch, tracking, reporting, and learner access.

00:00:42.484 --> 00:00:46.045
Common use cases include compliance training, technical training, onboarding, microlearning,

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:48.895
software training, product education, and scenario-based decision practice.

00:00:48.895 --> 00:00:52.100
These courses may look very different from one another.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.086
A short policy refresher might need a clean responsive structure and a simple quiz.

00:00:57.086 --> 00:01:00.292
A software workflow course might need demonstrations and practice.

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:03.497
A leadership scenario might need branching decisions and feedback.

00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:05.990
The tool does not replace instructional design.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:10.977
Strong courses begin with a reason for learning, a learner audience, a performance gap,

00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:12.401
and a clear outcome.

00:01:12.401 --> 00:01:15.607
Features matter, but features without purpose can create clutter.

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:19.525
The best Articulate 360 projects use the tool to support learning,

00:01:19.525 --> 00:01:22.730
not to show off every interaction or animation available.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:27.004
A useful way to frame Articulate 360 work is to treat each

00:01:27.004 --> 00:01:29.853
project as a learning product with a lifecycle.

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:33.415
It begins with a need, moves through design and development,

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:36.621
passes through review and testing, and eventually needs updates.

00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:40.895
Thinking in lifecycle terms keeps the team from focusing only on the

00:01:40.895 --> 00:01:42.675
initial build and forgetting maintainability,

00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:44.100
tracking, and learner support.
